<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michelangelo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1475">1475</a>–<a href="/years/1564">1564</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Michelangelo Buonarroti</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michelangelo Buonarroti (1475–1564) was a Florentine sculptor, painter, architect and poet, one of the greatest artists of the Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. His works include the marble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, the ceiling and Last Judgement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel">Sistine Chapel</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and the design of the dome of St Peter's Basilica. He personally chose much of his marble at the quarries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michelangelo.jpg" alt="Michelangelo" width="320" height="370" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Daniele da Volterra · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michelangelo_Daniele_da_Volterra_(dettaglio).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Michelangelo di Lodovico Buonarroti Simoni was born on 6 March 1475 in Caprese, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a> in Tuscany, where his father Ludovico was the town's judicial administrator. The family had for generations been small-scale bankers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, but the bank failed. Several months after his birth they returned to Florence. After his mother's death in 1481 he lived with a nanny and her husband, a stonecutter, in Settignano, where his father owned a marble quarry and a small farm. He later said he received the knack of handling chisel and hammer with the milk of his nurse.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Michelangelo achieved fame early, and two of his best-known works, the Pietà and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>, were sculpted before he was 30. Born in the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, he was mostly active in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from his thirties onward. Although he did not consider himself a painter, he created the scenes from Genesis on the ceiling of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel">Sistine Chapel</a> and The Last Judgment on its altar wall, two of the most influential frescoes in Western art.</p><p>His design of the Laurentian Library pioneered Mannerist architecture. At 71 he succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-da-sangallo-the-younger">Antonio da Sangallo the Younger</a> as architect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-peters-basilica">St. Peter's Basilica</a>, and he changed the plan so that the western end was finished to his design, as was the dome, with some modification after his death on 18 February 1564. He was the first Western artist whose biography was published in his lifetime: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giorgio-vasari">Giorgio Vasari</a>'s Lives of 1550 called him divino, and Ascanio Condivi's Life of 1553 was prepared with his participation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The Madonna of the Stairs is his earliest known work in marble, carved in shallow relief. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Bacchus</a>, a commission with a set subject, has the traditional wine cup, vine wreath and fawn, but shows the god with bleary eyes and an unsteady stance. In the Madonna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> the Christ Child, held by his mother's hand, is about to step off into the world. His contemporaries admired his terribilità, the ability to instil awe in those who saw his art.</p><p>He wrote more than three hundred sonnets and madrigals. He was a devout Catholic whose faith deepened at the end of his life. Attempts by later artists to imitate the expressive physicality of his style helped bring about Mannerism. The dome of St. Peter's influenced church building for centuries, including St Paul's Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, and his unfinished giants for the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> had a deep effect on sculptors such as Rodin and Henry Moore.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Michelangelo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…artistic capital of Europe: Sixtus IV built the Sistine Chapel and founded the Vatican Library, Julius II commissioned <b>Michelangelo</b>’s ceiling of the Sistine Chapel and Raphael’s frescoes in the Vatican, and the new basilica of Saint Peter, begun in 1506, was completed by Carlo Maderno and Gian Lorenzo…</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The last republic defended itself during the siege of 1529–1530, with fortifications designed by <b>Michelangelo</b>, until Francesco Ferrucci’s relief army was destroyed at Gavinana.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…of medieval Europe and St Peter’s in Rome; Christian art inspired the icons of Byzantium, the paintings of the Renaissance masters and the sculpture of <b>Michelangelo</b>; and Christian music, from Gregorian chant to the works of Bach and Handel, is at the foundation of the Western musical tradition.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>According to Vasari and Condivi, <b>Michelangelo</b> painted standing, not lying on his back as the biographer Paolo Giovio imagined, and the final narrative scene, God in the act of creation, was painted in a single day.</blockquote><a href="/work/sistine-chapel-ceiling">Sistine Chapel ceiling · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1505 Julius II decided to demolish the ancient basilica altogether, partly to house the enormous tomb <b>Michelangelo</b> was to design for him.</blockquote><a href="/work/st-peters-basilica">St. Peter's Basilica · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Building took 120 years and drew on <b>Michelangelo</b>, Maderno and Bernini.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-18">18 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Caprese Michelangelo, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.64,11.99&amp;year=1532" aria-label="Caprese Michelangelo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Michelangelo"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5736 3806h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5736 3806h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.64,11.99&amp;year=1532">What was here in 1532? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The best of artists hath no thought to show Which the rough stone in its superfluous shell Doth not include: to break the marble spell Is all the hand that serves the brain can do.&#8221;</p><footer>Sonnet 15 &#183; tr. John Addington Symonds, 1878 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10314/pg10314-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Now hath my life across a stormy sea Like a frail bark reached that wide port where all Are bidden, ere the final reckoning fall Of good and evil for eternity.&#8221;</p><footer>Sonnet 65, to Giorgio Vasari, on the brink of death &#183; tr. John Addington Symonds, 1878 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10314/pg10314-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Sweet is my sleep, but more to be mere stone, So long as ruin and dishonour reign; To bear nought, to feel nought, is my great gain; Then wake me not, speak in an undertone!&#8221;</p><footer>The Speech of Night (epigram on his statue of Night) &#183; tr. John Addington Symonds, 1878 &#183; spoken by his statue of Night, in answer to an admirer's verses &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10314/pg10314-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;From heaven his spirit came, and robed in clay The realms of justice and of mercy trod, Then rose a living man to gaze on God, That he might make the truth as clear as day.&#8221;</p><footer>Sonnet on Dante &#183; tr. John Addington Symonds, 1878 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10314/pg10314-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;My beard turns up to heaven; my nape falls in, Fixed on my spine: my breast-bone visibly Grows like a harp: a rich embroidery Bedews my face from brush-drops thick and thin.&#8221;</p><footer>Sonnet to Giovanni da Pistoia, on painting the Sistine Chapel ceiling &#183; tr. John Addington Symonds, 1878 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10314/pg10314-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
